# A one-layer image whose only job is to make the upstream image runnable as a non-root uid.
#
# beardedio/terraria is built to run as root and cannot be persuaded out of it from the manifest
# side. Its entrypoint (`run-vanilla.sh`) runs under `set -euo pipefail` and hardcodes an
# absolute HOME rather than $HOME:
#
#     mkdir -p /root/.local/share/Terraria
#     ln -sT /config /root/.local/share/Terraria/Worlds
#
# Debian's /root is 0700 root:root, so a non-root uid cannot traverse it. Deployed with
# `runAsUser: 1000` against the stock image on 2026-08-31 the pod CrashLoopBackOff'd on
#
#     mkdir: cannot create directory '/root': Permission denied
#
# — at the entrypoint's first filesystem write, nowhere near the world data, which is why an
# analysis of the volume's ownership did not predict it.
#
# Neither manifest-side workaround rescues it, and both were considered. `fsGroup` acts on the
# mounted volume and /root is image rootfs. Shadowing /root with an emptyDir hides
# `/root/My Games/Terraria/favorites.json`, which the upstream Dockerfile seeds there
# specifically to fix an error ("fix for favorites.json error", its words), so it trades this
# bug for that one plus a root init container.
#
# DIGEST-PINNED, and doubly load-bearing here: this image holds the one irreplaceable dataset in
# the fleet. The pin sits on `vanilla-latest`, a MUTABLE tag, so the digest is the entire pin and
# Renovate's dockerfile manager raises a digest PR when the tag re-points; keep the tag alongside
# the digest so the channel is still legible. A bump is realised by a rebuild, never by a pull:
# see defaults/main.yml.
#
# beardedio ALSO publishes an immutable versioned tag per release — `vanilla-1.4.5.7`,
# `vanilla-1.4.5.6`, back to `vanilla-1.4.1.2` (read off the Docker Hub tag list, 2026-09-02).
# This comment said the opposite until then, and the correction is operational rather than
# cosmetic: pinning back to a known-good game version is a clean tag pin, not a bare digest.
# That is remediation option 2 on issue #822, where a connect-and-drop TCP probe against
# 1.4.5.7 crashes the server loop and 1.4.5.6 takes the same probe without incident. It stays
# an option rather than the fix because 1.4.5.7 has already saved this world, and a 1.4.5.6
# server may refuse or damage a world it wrote.
FROM beardedio/terraria:vanilla-latest@sha256:2b305d0eabbba11d14545159b015eb9f5d15a003ae7d90f6b0bb839cbce37773

# The whole fix. Two paths, for two different reasons:
#
#   /root     the hardcoded HOME above. Left at 0700 rather than widened to 0755 — owned by
#             1000 it is already traversable by the only uid that needs it, and 0700 is the
#             tighter of the two.
#   /vanilla  the WORKDIR. The server prints "Error Logging Enabled" at startup and writes its
#             error log relative to the working directory, so a read-only /vanilla would fail
#             the first time it actually had something to report — the worst possible moment.
#
# Chowning /vanilla lets the app rewrite its own binaries, which is worth stating plainly. It is
# still strictly LESS privilege than the status quo: today PID 1 is root and can rewrite every
# path in the container, this one included.
RUN chown -R 1000:1000 /root /vanilla
